From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Niklas Sheth <hi@niklassheth.com>
Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 20:18:59 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] drm/asahi: G16: provision first-use resources in SUBMIT;
 publish without per-Work allocation

Publication runs in the job worker that signals completion fences. A
failure there can only fail the queue after SUBMIT has returned, and a
GFP_KERNEL allocation there can recurse into reclaim that waits on those
fences. Other drivers allocate and validate in the submit ioctl.

- SUBMIT now creates a queue's firmware rings and a VM root's TVB pool,
  which its first publication used to create. Their ENOMEM/ENOSPC return
  from the ioctl with nothing queued and the queue left usable.
  Publication still finds or creates them, through the same helpers.
- The TA, fragment and compute Work encodings are scratch buffers
  allocated once with the runtime instead of per Work; publication is
  serialized by the runtime lock and copies them into firmware memory.
- A render's firmware write batch is computed on demand instead of being
  collected into a vector (or a stack list: the job worker's stack is
  tight), and update_batch takes its ranges the same way.
- The retire log gets its full capacity at init, and flights are pushed
  into the slot reserve_flight reserved.
- A Work encoding that fails for ioctl-validated parameters is a driver
  bug: EIO with a message, not EINVAL.

The publish path's deepest frames total about 160 bytes less than before.
Amortized capacity growth in reserve_flight, Work arena refills, scratch
leases, client alias page tables and TVB growth still allocate in the
worker; see the design note.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
